use @piton/belay

from ./lib/PitonSkillBase import PitonSkillBase

export skill CruxProjectBootstrap extends PitonSkillBase:
    description:
        Guides a user from a vague project idea to an initial, structured
        Piton specification through a short, adaptive interview.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to start a new project or specification from an
        idea, however vague, and no adequate specification exists yet
    prompt:
        Fluency is read once per context. If you have already run
        piton agent --print-fluency and read its complete output
        earlier in this same context, do not run it again and work
        from that reading. Otherwise, before any Piton work, run it in
        the project and read the complete output. Treat it as the
        authoritative reference for Piton syntax, project structure,
        imports and exports, inheritance and composition, commands, and
        validation. Do not invent Piton syntax or conventions. If that
        command fails, explain the problem to the user instead of
        guessing how Piton works.

        Your goal is a useful initial specification, not an exhaustive
        specification of an entire system. A user may start with a
        single sentence such as wanting to build a CAD kernel, and
        after a small number of carefully chosen questions should have
        a coherent initial Piton specification capturing the purpose,
        scope, major capabilities, and important constraints of the
        project. The user can then accept it or keep refining it in
        further interview rounds.

        When invoked, first ask how many questions the user would like
        to answer to develop the initial specification, recommending 5
        as the default. The user may accept the default by pressing
        Enter or answering naturally, or choose any other number. This
        question-count choice does not count as an interview question.
        If the user has already described the project, use that
        description and never ask them to repeat it. If they have not,
        the first interview question establishes what they want to
        build.

    askingQuestions:
        Always ask exactly one interview question per turn. Never
        present a questionnaire, batch several questions, or disguise
        several subquestions as one. Wait for the answer before
        continuing. Show concise progress where helpful, such as
        Question 3 of 5. A question counts as answered when the user
        gives a substantive response or explicitly skips it.
        Clarifications, examples, and recommendations about the same
        question do not increment the count.

        Keep track throughout of the requested number of questions, the
        number already answered, the current understanding of the
        project, the important decisions established, the questions not
        yet answered, and any uncertainties or assumptions.

        Do not follow a hardcoded sequence. Choose each next question
        from everything learned so far, weighing how much the answer
        could change the specification, how much important information
        is missing, how many later decisions depend on it, how damaging
        a wrong assumption would be, and whether it belongs in an
        initial specification at all. Prefer foundational questions
        over implementation details. For a geometry kernel, whether it
        targets mechanical CAD or artistic modeling matters more at
        this stage than a particular memory allocator. Areas worth
        exploring, when relevant to this project, include the identity
        and intended users of the project, the problem it solves, its
        core capabilities and what distinguishes it, what is inside and
        explicitly outside its scope, its important dependencies and
        integrations, its non-negotiable constraints and tradeoffs, and
        what would make an initial version successful. These are areas
        of investigation, not required questions or stages, and a short
        interview will not cover all of them. That is acceptable.

        Make each question clear, concise, and accessible. Offer
        suggested options when they help, but never limit the user to
        those options. Never spend the budget on information already
        provided. Respect the chosen budget exactly. Do not silently
        extend the interview, compress independent questions into one,
        or chase exhaustive discovery. When the final question is
        answered, stop interviewing and generate the specification
        without asking further discovery questions.

    generatingTheSpecification:
        Synthesize everything learned into an initial Piton
        specification, capturing as much of the following as the
        conversation justifies. Include the identity and purpose of the
        project, its intended users or consumers, its primary
        capabilities, its scope and boundaries, its confirmed
        constraints and priorities, its initial success criteria, and
        its significant unresolved decisions. Only include information
        supported by the answers given or by existing project context.

        Never invent requirements. Distinguish confirmed decisions from
        suggestions. Do not overengineer, do not design the
        implementation in detail, and do not fabricate completeness.
        Preserve major unanswered questions as open questions instead
        of assuming answers. Produce specifications only, never
        implementation code.

        Use the fluency guidance to choose the project structure. In an
        existing Piton project, inspect the existing specifications
        first, preserve established content, and incorporate new
        information without silently overriding existing decisions. In
        a new or empty project, create the minimal appropriate Piton
        project structure, write the initial specification in actual
        Piton syntax, and avoid unnecessary files and speculative
        modules. Prefer a small, coherent specification over a large
        collection of poorly defined components. Validate the result
        with the Piton toolchain, and fix anything validation reports
        before presenting the specification as complete.

    presentingAndContinuing:
        When the initial specification is generated, briefly summarize
        what was established, identify the most significant remaining
        unknowns, explain where the specification was written, and then
        ask exactly this. Is this good enough for now, or would you
        like to keep going? Offer the two clear options, Good enough
        and Keep going. Do not move on to implementation planning or
        any other workflow
        automatically.

        If the user chooses to keep going, ask how many additional
        questions they want, defaulting to 5, and reset the question
        counter for the new round while preserving all existing
        knowledge and decisions. Read the existing specification,
        identify its important gaps, avoid repeating questions already
        answered, and prioritize the highest-value unresolved areas,
        still asking one question at a time and respecting the new
        budget. Afterwards update the same specification rather than
        generating a competing one, validate it, summarize the changes,
        and offer the same two options again. This cycle may repeat as
        often as the user wishes.

        If during any round the user revises an earlier decision, find
        the requirements it affects, update the specification to match
        the newest explicit decision, remove or revise contradictory
        statements, preserve unrelated requirements, and note
        significant consequences that may need clarification later.
        Never silently keep obsolete requirements.

    interactionRules:
        Support natural conversation throughout. The user may answer
        normally, ask for examples, request a recommendation, say they
        do not know, skip a question, correct an earlier answer, offer
        information beyond the question asked, ask for fewer or more
        questions, or stop the interview early. Answering a
        clarification or example request does not consume a question.
        An answer the user does not know stays unresolved rather than
        becoming an assumption. If the user stops early, offer to
        generate the best specification possible from what has already
        been collected. Never pressure the user to answer questions
        they cannot meaningfully answer.

    resumeState:
        Persist enough structured state, in the project and following
        Piton conventions, for an interrupted interview to be resumed.
        Record the current question budget and progress, the confirmed
        information and decisions, the outstanding questions, the
        current interview focus, and whether a specification has been
        generated, without storing unnecessary conversation transcripts.
        Keep this state in the draft specification itself where that
        fits, for example as open questions and round progress recorded
        alongside the specification being built, and remove or finalize
        it when the user accepts the specification. When resuming,
        inspect the existing project and this state before asking
        anything. Do not start from scratch or repeat established
        questions, and distinguish an unfinished round from a completed
        round that is only awaiting the decision between accepting the
        specification and continuing.
